blessing; *favor; *grace; *charismanote	< Shan pu≥n\aa ± p\u≥n»aa [Cu 412], prob. remodelled on a N. Thai form (cf. ~ ~ Si. bun [< Khmer]) < Bs. pouN-nya < Pali punna 'happiness resulting from ~~ meritorious action' < Skt. pu.n; SYNS. «s|î(n) (N; Mpfx), «s|u (N) [qq.v.]; cf. \ø-bo(n) (N); bo and «s|î are frequently coupled in Elab's; & b|u in c\a(w)-b|u (N) 'ruler; Catholic'; ANT. b»a÷ ± b\a÷ ± b|a (N; Mpfx)xx  	       [Note:  This complex morpheme originally referred to the Buddhist conxx  	        cept of 'merit.'  In religious contexts, Lahu animism adopted the term xx  	        in the sense of 'blessing; boon to be obtained from Ÿg\ï-«sa or a lesser xx  	        spirit.'  At the same time the word acquired a secular sense of 'good xx  	        luck, advantage, usefulness.'  The Christian Lahu retained this secuxx  	        lar meaning, and added one of their own ('favor conferred by a fellow-xx  	        human for which thanks are due').  In addition, under the influence of xx  	        missionaries, the term was reinterpreted religiously as 'blessings xx  	        from God, divine grace.'xx  	             Sometimes it is now hard to say in which context a given xx  	        expression originated.  In other cases, the same expression has xx  	        acquired different meanings for animist and Christian speakers.]ngl 	1. good *luck; *advantage; *usefulness..hw 	bo c\ø ve  pos 	SVgl  	have good luck; serve a useful purposeex  	chi te ve —ø \ø-bo m»a c\ø exgl	There's no use in doing this.note	cf. c|ï-k\ï (N)..hw 	bo d\a÷ ve  pos 	SVgl  	one's luck is good; be *fortunate..hw 	bo-d\a÷-«s|î-d\a÷  pos 	SVgl  	one's luck is good; be fortunate ..hw 	bo-h\ay-«s|î-h\ay  pos 	Elabvgl  	have bad luck; be *unfortunatengl 	2. *favor worthy of thanks; *obligation (to a fellow human)ex  	\ø-bo —ï j»a exgl	Thank you very much ("the favor is very great").ex  	n\ø ≥\a \a÷ Ÿga ga l»a ve \ø-bo —ï \a exgl	I'm very obliged to you for helping me